Abstract

This thesis constructs a mathematical framework for intelligence conceived as a distributed, scale‑free phenomenon. Intelligence is defined as the capacity of a dynamical system to reduce uncertainty across spatiotemporal scales while maintaining stability. The formalism rests on three pillars: (i) the information geometry of belief updating, (ii) a field‑theoretic description of multi‑agent systems, and (iii) Lyapunov‑based stability analysis of collective cognition. Four theorems are proved:

Theorem 1 (Distributed Epistemic Convergence). For a network of Bayesian agents communicating on a graph, the collective epistemic energy converges to zero under sparse communication, while above a critical communication frequency a pitchfork bifurcation yields malignant epistemic herding, wherein agents synchronise to a shared but false belief. The normal‑form analysis is carried out explicitly for the two‑agent Gaussian case, and the extension to N agents is established via centre‑manifold reduction.

Theorem 2 (Stability of Ethical Attractors). An ethically constrained distributed intelligence equipped with local architectural halting monitors possesses a Lyapunov‑stable attractor within the safety manifold. Global verification of alignment is undecidable for sufficiently expressive centralised systems, but the distributed architecture reduces safety to a conjunction of polynomially‑checkable local conditions, rendering the global safety property computationally tractable.

Theorem 3 (Collective Intelligence Phase Transition). Using a mean‑field reduction, a population of cognitive agents undergoes a continuous, non‑equilibrium phase transition to emergent collective cognition when the filling fraction of cognitive maps exceeds a critical density ρ_c. The transition is shown to belong to the 2D Ising universality class, with order‑parameter exponent β = 1/8. A discrete Z₂ symmetry is broken at the transition, and the absence of a continuous symmetry implies that the ordered phase supports a spectral gap rather than Goldstone modes.

Theorem 4 (Entropic Collapse Criterion). The centralisation of epistemic influence reduces collective entropy below a viability threshold, causing the spectral gap of the belief‑update Fokker–Planck operator to vanish and the system to become irreversibly trapped in a low‑diversity attractor. The intelligence density functional ℐ = CE/H is shown to attain its unique maximum at an intermediate degree of centralisation, thereby establishing a mathematical necessity for distributed architectures. Axiomatisation of ℐ via Shannon–Khinchin principles demonstrates uniqueness up to a multiplicative constant.

The work integrates and extends the Free Energy Principle (Friston 2019; Ramstead et al. 2023), Amari’s information geometry, mean‑field game theory, and recent undecidability results in AI alignment (Melo et al. 2025). An intelligence density functional is derived and connected to Fisher information via the Cramér–Rao bound. The mathematical structure demonstrates that ethical stability is a geometric invariant of sustainable cognitive fields.

