1. State Spaces of Intelligence

1.1 Dynamical Definition

An intelligence is modelled by a state vector s_t \in \mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n evolving according to the Itô stochastic differential equation

ds_t = f(s_t, a_t)\,dt + \sigma(s_t)\,dW_t,

where a_t \in \mathcal{A} is the control action, W_t is a standard Wiener process, and the drift function f: \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{A} \to \mathbb{R}^n is assumed Lipschitz in s uniformly in a to guarantee existence and uniqueness of strong solutions. The diffusion coefficient \sigma is bounded and non‑degenerate, ensuring that the process explores the full state space.

Definition 1.1 (Local Intelligence). Let an agent maintain an approximate posterior q_t(s) over hidden states. The local intelligence is the negative time‑average of variational free energy:

\mathcal{I}_{\text{loc}} = -\lim_{T\to\infty} \frac{1}{T} \int_0^T F(q_t, o_t)\,dt,

where

F(q, o) = D_{\text{KL}}[q(s) \,\|\, p(s)] - \mathbb{E}_{q}[\ln p(o \mid s)].

Here p(s) is a prior distribution and p(o \mid s) is the likelihood of observation o given state s. Larger \mathcal{I}_{\text{loc}} corresponds to a system that consistently maintains a lower free energy.

1.2 Distributed and Collective Intelligence

Consider a network of N agents with adjacency matrix A_{ij} \ge 0. Communication is modelled as a message‑passing process whereby agent j receives a sufficient statistic of agent i's belief.

Definition 1.2 (Distributed Intelligence).

\mathcal{I}_{\text{dist}} = \sum_{i=1}^N \mathcal{I}_{\text{loc}}^{(i)} + \sum_{i,j} A_{ij}\, \mathcal{I}_{\text{com}}^{(i \to j)},

where \mathcal{I}_{\text{com}}^{(i \to j)} is the information gain (in nats) provided to agent j upon receiving a message from i, weighted by the reliability of the channel.

Definition 1.3 (Collective Cognition). Collective cognition is present when the joint belief distribution q(\mathbf{s}) = q(s^1, \dots, s^N) cannot be factorised over agents. Its degree is quantified by the multi‑information:

\mathcal{C} = \sum_{i=1}^N H[q(s^i)] - H[q(\mathbf{s})],

where H is the Shannon entropy. If \mathcal{C} = 0, the agents are epistemically independent; positive \mathcal{C} measures the emergence of shared information.

Definition 1.4 (Hierarchical Cognition). If the joint distribution is structured by a directed acyclic graph \mathcal{T} representing levels of abstraction, the hierarchical cognition is the sum of mutual informations along parent‑child edges.

