2. Information Geometry of Cognition

2.1 The Statistical Manifold

For an agent, a belief is a probability distribution q_\theta(s) parameterised by \theta \in \Theta \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d. The set of all such distributions forms a smooth Riemannian manifold \mathcal{M} equipped with the Fisher information metric:

g_{ij}(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_{q_\theta}\!\left[\partial_i \ln q_\theta \, \partial_j \ln q_\theta\right].

This metric is invariant under reparameterisation (Amari 2016). The Fisher information matrix G(\theta) = [g_{ij}(\theta)] plays the role of the metric tensor.

Definition 2.1 (Free Energy Gradient Flow). For a generative model p(o, s), the variational free energy is

F(\theta) = D_{\text{KL}}[q_\theta(s) \,\|\, p(s)] - \mathbb{E}_{q_\theta}[\ln p(o \mid s)].

The natural gradient descent on F is

\dot{\theta} = -\tilde{\nabla}_\theta F(\theta) = -G(\theta)^{-1} \nabla_\theta F(\theta).

This flow is the foundation of Bayesian mechanics (Ramstead et al. 2023): perception and action are both instances of free‑energy minimisation on the statistical manifold.

Proposition 2.1 (Free Energy Bounds Geodesic Distance). For two nearby beliefs parameterised by \theta_1 and \theta_2,

F(\theta_2) - F(\theta_1) \ge \frac{1}{2} d_{\mathcal{M}}^2(\theta_1, \theta_2) + O(d^3),

where d_{\mathcal{M}} is the Riemannian geodesic distance on \mathcal{M} induced by the Fisher–Rao metric.

Proof. Expand F(\theta_2) in a Taylor series about \theta_1. The gradient of F vanishes at the optimum, and the Hessian is the Fisher information matrix G(\theta_1). The geodesic distance satisfies d_{\mathcal{M}}^2(\theta_1, \theta_2) = (\theta_2 - \theta_1)^\top G(\theta_1) (\theta_2 - \theta_1) + O(|\theta_2 - \theta_1|^3). The inequality follows by comparing the Taylor expansion with the geodesic expansion. ∎

2.2 Key Information‑Theoretic Objects

Epistemic Divergence: The divergence between agents i and j is

\mathcal{D}_{\text{epis}}(i, j) = D_{\text{KL}}[q^{(i)}(\mathbf{s}) \,\|\, q^{(j)}(\mathbf{s})].

Collective Entropy:

H_{\text{coll}} = H[q(\mathbf{s})] = -\int q(\mathbf{s}) \ln q(\mathbf{s})\,d\mathbf{s}.

Epistemic Coupling:

\mathcal{K}_{ij} = I(s^i; s^j) = D_{\text{KL}}[q(s^i, s^j) \,\|\, q(s^i) q(s^j)].

Civilisation Uncertainty: For a population with influence weights w_i, the civilisation uncertainty is the entropy of the mixture distribution

\bar{q}(\mathbf{s}) = \sum_i w_i q^{(i)}(\mathbf{s}),

which measures the overall epistemic diversity of the system.

2.3 The Amari–Friston Bridge

Amari's natural gradient (1998) and Friston's free‑energy minimisation are equivalent descriptions of the same dynamics. The natural gradient flow of free energy on \mathcal{M} is the Riemannian gradient flow with respect to the Fisher–Rao metric. This provides the rigorous geometric foundation: learning is a geodesic motion on \mathcal{M}, driven by prediction error.

In the multi‑agent extension (Friston et al. 2024), each agent follows a local natural gradient while exchanging sufficient statistics with neighbours:

\dot{\theta}^{(i)} = -\tilde{\nabla}_{\theta^{(i)}} F_i(\theta^{(i)}) + \sum_{j \in \mathcal{N}(i)} A_{ij}\, \Gamma(\theta^{(i)}, \theta^{(j)}),

where \Gamma is a communication operator that aligns beliefs. This coupled gradient flow is the starting point for our cognitive field theory.

2.4 Markov Blankets and Self‑Evidencing

A single agent can be delineated by a Markov blanket. Let the total state space be partitioned into internal (s_{\text{int}}), external (s_{\text{ext}}), sensory (s_{\text{sens}}), and active (s_{\text{act}}) states. A Markov blanket exists if

p(s_{\text{int}}, s_{\text{ext}} \mid s_{\text{sens}}, s_{\text{act}}) = p(s_{\text{int}} \mid s_{\text{sens}}, s_{\text{act}})\, p(s_{\text{ext}} \mid s_{\text{sens}}, s_{\text{act}}).

Lemma 2.1 (Free Energy Lemma, Ramstead et al. 2023). For any random dynamical system with a Markov blanket, the flow of internal states can be expressed as a gradient flow on variational free energy. The system is self‑evidencing: it acts to maximise the evidence for its own existence.

In a multi‑agent setting, each agent maintains its own blanket. Collective cognition emerges when blankets become partially transparent: agents share sensory states or posterior parameters, effectively creating a higher‑order Markov blanket at the population level.

