3. Multi‑Agent Cognitive Fields

3.1 Continuum Limit

Let N agents be located at positions x_k \in \Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^2. Each agent possesses a belief field \psi_k(x, t) describing its internal model of latent causes extended over space. We assume Gaussian beliefs:

\psi_k(x, t) = \mathcal{N}(\mu_k(t), \Sigma_k(t)),

and define the collective cognitive field as the weighted superposition:

\Phi(x, t) = \sum_{k=1}^N w_k(t)\, \psi_k(x, t),

where w_k(t) are dynamic influence weights. In the mean‑field limit N \to \infty with spatial density \rho, the sum converges to an integral:

\Phi(x, t) = \int_\Omega w(y, t)\, \psi(x \mid y, t)\, \rho(y)\, dy.

3.2 Field Equations

Under the assumptions of Gaussian beliefs and nearest‑neighbour coupling, the mean field \mu(x, t) satisfies a stochastic reaction‑diffusion equation on L^2(\Omega):

\partial_t \Phi = D \nabla^2 \Phi + \alpha \Phi (1 - \Phi \star K) + \sigma \xi(x, t),

where \star denotes spatial convolution with an influence kernel K(r) = \exp(-r^2/(4\lambda^2)), D > 0 is a diffusion coefficient, \alpha is a growth/saturation parameter, and \xi is spatiotemporal white noise. This equation is structurally identical to Model A dynamics in the Hohenberg–Halperin classification (Hohenberg & Halperin 1977).

The operator A = D\nabla^2 on the Sobolev space H^1(\Omega) generates an analytic semigroup (Henry 1981), guaranteeing well‑posedness of the deterministic part. With additive white noise, the stochastic evolution is well‑posed in the mild sense (Da Prato & Zabczyk 2014).

3.3 Connection to Mean‑Field Game Theory

Mean‑field game (MFG) theory models large populations of non‑cooperative agents. In the kinetic MFG formulation of Grover & Huo (2024), each agent minimises a cost function that depends on its own state and the mean field generated by others. The resulting coupled forward‑backward system—Fokker–Planck equation for the density evolution and Hamilton–Jacobi–Bellman equation for the value function—exhibits a phase transition from a homogeneous equilibrium to travelling wave solutions.

Our cognitive field reduces to an MFG when each agent minimises its local variational free energy subject to the mean field \Phi. The self‑consistency condition derived below (Theorem 3) is the fixed‑point equation of this game‑theoretic coupling.

