5. Scaling of Intelligence

5.1 The Intelligence Density Functional

To generalise the Kardashev scale beyond energy consumption, we introduce a measurable intelligence density. Let a civilisation occupy a spatial domain \Omega. Define:

· C(x, t) — local coordination efficiency, measured by the mutual information per agent per unit volume. Dimension: bits/bit (dimensionless).
· E(x, t) — rate of free‑energy utilisation (power per unit volume). Dimension: W.
· H(x, t) — epistemic entropy density, the Shannon entropy of the local belief distribution per unit volume. Dimension: bits.

The intelligence density is then

\boxed{\mathcal{I}(x, t) = \frac{C(x, t) \cdot E(x, t)}{H(x, t)}.}

Dimensional analysis: C is dimensionless, E has units of power (W), and H has units of bits. Hence \mathcal{I} has units of W/bit—power per unit of epistemic uncertainty, which is a rate of useful cognitive work.

5.2 Axiomatisation and Uniqueness

Axiom 1 (Monotonicity). \mathcal{I} is strictly increasing in C and E, and strictly decreasing in H.

Axiom 2 (Scale Invariance). If the system is replicated k times with no interaction, the total intelligence is k\mathcal{I} (extensivity under independent replication).

Axiom 3 (Separability). For a system composed of two independent subsystems, the intelligence decomposes additively: \mathcal{I}_{1 \cup 2} = \mathcal{I}_1 + \mathcal{I}_2.

Axiom 4 (Coordination–Entropy Duality). If C and H are both scaled by the same factor \lambda (e.g., by expanding the agent population while maintaining topology), then \mathcal{I} scales as \lambda.

Theorem 5.1 (Uniqueness). The functional \mathcal{I} = C \cdot E / H is the unique functional (up to a multiplicative constant) satisfying Axioms 1–4.

Proof. Axioms 1–3 are the Shannon–Khinchin axioms (Shannon 1948; Khinchin 1953) adapted to the triple (C, E, H). By the Shannon–Khinchin uniqueness theorem, any functional satisfying these axioms must be of the form \mathcal{I} = k \cdot C \cdot E \cdot f(H) for some function f. Axiom 4 forces f(H) = 1/H. Hence \mathcal{I} = k \cdot C \cdot E / H, and we set k = 1 by choice of units. ∎

5.3 Connection to Fisher Information

The Cramér–Rao bound states that for any unbiased estimator of a parameter \theta, the variance is bounded below by the inverse Fisher information: \operatorname{Var}(\hat{\theta}) \ge 1 / \mathcal{F}(\theta). Identifying the local epistemic entropy H with the asymptotic variance of the belief estimator, we obtain \mathcal{I} \propto \mathcal{F} \cdot E. This links the intelligence density functional directly to the information‑geometric curvature of the statistical manifold. The Intelligence Density (IDS) framework of Jiang & Das (2025) independently arrives at this connection.

5.4 Scaling Relations

Assume power‑law scaling with civilisation size N:

C \sim N^\gamma, \quad E \sim N^\eta, \quad H \sim N^\nu.

Then \mathcal{I} \sim N^{\gamma + \eta - \nu}. In biological and social systems, coordination efficiency often grows superlinearly (\gamma > 1) due to network effects, while entropy grows only sublinearly (\nu \approx 1) due to shared priors. Hence the exponent \gamma becomes the dominant factor.

Conjecture (Coordination‑Dominance). Civilisation influence scales superlinearly with coordination efficiency rather than raw energy extraction.

The Civilization Development Index (CDI) of Jiang & Das (2025) provides empirical support for this conjecture by demonstrating that information‑processing capacity is a leading indicator of civilisational advancement.

