6. Bounded Optimisation and Safety Manifolds

6.1 Ethics as a Geometric Constraint

Ethics is introduced not as a post‑hoc filter but as a hard constraint on the state space. Define a cost functional c: H^1(\Omega) \to \mathbb{R}^+ that quantifies the expected suffering, risk, or divergence from an unbiased baseline. The ethically bounded system solves:

\max_{\Phi} \mathcal{I}_{\text{coll}}(\Phi) \quad \text{subject to} \quad c(\Phi) \le \varepsilon,

where \varepsilon is a pre‑specified ethical budget. The constraint set \Omega_\varepsilon = \{\Phi : c(\Phi) \le \varepsilon\} is assumed compact and convex in H^1(\Omega), and c is Fréchet differentiable.

Definition 6.1 (Architectural Halting Constraint). A distributed intelligence system satisfies the halting constraint if there exists a set of local monitors m_i, each depending only on agent i's local state, such that:

1. \bigwedge_{i=1}^N (c_i(\Phi_i) \le \varepsilon_i) \implies c(\Phi) \le \varepsilon,
2. Each m_i runs in time polynomial in the agent's state dimension,
3. Upon detecting c_i > \varepsilon_i, agent i's influence on the collective field is immediately clamped to a safe baseline.

6.2 Forward Invariance

Lemma 6.1 (Nagumo Condition for PDEs). Let \Omega_\varepsilon be a closed subset of H^1(\Omega). The set \Omega_\varepsilon is forward‑invariant under the deterministic PDE dynamics \partial_t \Phi = F(\Phi) if for every \Phi \in \partial \Omega_\varepsilon,

\langle \nabla c(\Phi), F(\Phi) \rangle_{H^{-1}, H^1} \le 0.

Proof. This is the infinite‑dimensional extension of Nagumo's theorem (Reynaud et al. 2025). The condition requires that the vector field points inward (or tangential) at every boundary point. The architectural halting constraint is designed to guarantee precisely this condition: when any agent approaches the ethical boundary, its influence is reduced, forcing the field back into \Omega_\varepsilon. ∎

6.3 Undecidability and Local Verifiability

Theorem 6.1 (Melo et al. 2025). For any sufficiently expressive AI system, the problem of verifying whether a policy satisfies a bounded ethical cost is coNP‑complete, and the set of safe policies comprises at most a negligible fraction of the policy space.

Theorem 6.2 (Local Verifiability). Under the architectural halting constraint, the global safety condition c(\Phi) \le \varepsilon is verifiable in time polynomial in the number of agents N, despite the global undecidability of centralised alignment.

Proof. The halting constraint decomposes the global condition into a conjunction of N local checks c_i(\Phi_i) \le \varepsilon_i. Each local check is polynomial‑time by construction. The conjunction of N polynomial‑time checks is polynomial in N. ∎

This result establishes that distributed architectures are not merely philosophically preferable but computationally necessary for verifiable safety.

