7. Theorem 1 — Distributed Epistemic Convergence

7.1 Setup

Let N agents communicate on a connected, undirected graph with Laplacian L. The algebraic connectivity is \lambda_2(L) > 0. We assume the graph is an expander, so \lambda_2(L) \ge \lambda_0 > 0 uniformly in N (Hoory, Linial & Wigderson 2006).

Each agent i maintains a belief p_i^t \in \Delta^{K}, the probability simplex over K states. The belief update consists of two alternating steps:

1. Local Bayesian update: Agent i receives a noisy observation and updates its belief via Bayes rule with learning rate \alpha_t satisfying \sum \alpha_t = \infty, \sum \alpha_t^2 < \infty.
2. Communication: With probability \omega, agent i averages its belief with neighbours:

p_i^{t+1} = (1 - \varepsilon) p_i^t + \frac{\varepsilon}{\deg(i)} \sum_{j \in \mathcal{N}(i)} p_j^t + \varepsilon \xi_t^i,

where \varepsilon \in (0, 1) is the trust parameter and \xi_t^i is bounded additive noise (\|\xi_t^i\|_1 \le \delta).

The true state of the world yields the objective belief p^*.

Definition 7.1 (Collective Epistemic Energy).

E(t) = \frac{1}{N^2} \sum_{i,j} \|p_i^t - p_j^t\|_1^2.

Definition 7.2 (Log‑Sobolev Inequality). A probability distribution q on a Riemannian manifold satisfies a log‑Sobolev inequality (LSI) with constant \rho > 0 if for all smooth functions f,

\operatorname{Ent}_q[f^2] \le \frac{2}{\rho} \mathbb{E}_q[\|\nabla f\|^2].

The LSI implies exponential convergence of the Langevin diffusion in KL divergence (Vempala & Wibisono 2019; Altschuler & Chewi 2024).

7.2 Assumptions

A1 (Log‑Sobolev). Each agent's posterior distribution satisfies an LSI with constant \rho > 0 uniform across agents and time.

A2 (Expander Graph). The communication graph has algebraic connectivity \lambda_2(L) \ge \lambda_0 > 0.

A3 (Bounded Noise). \|\xi_t^i\|_1 \le \delta for all i, t.

7.3 Statement

Theorem 1. There exists a critical communication frequency

\omega^* = \frac{\rho \lambda_0}{2\delta}

such that:

· (i) If \omega < \omega^*, then E(t) \to 0 almost surely as t \to \infty, and the consensus belief converges to the true posterior p^*.
· (ii) If \omega > \omega^*, a pitchfork bifurcation occurs: a new stable fixed point \hat{p} \neq p^* appears, and the original fixed point p^* loses stability. Consequently, for generic initial conditions, agents converge to the false belief \hat{p} — the malignant epistemic herding (MEH) regime.

7.4 Proof

Lemma 7.1 (Consensus Contraction). In the absence of observations, the communication step contracts the collective epistemic energy:

\mathbb{E}[E(t+1) \mid E(t)] \le (1 - \varepsilon \lambda_2(L)) E(t) + O(\delta^2).

Proof. This is standard: the consensus update is a linear dynamical system whose contraction rate is governed by the spectral gap of the graph Laplacian (Olshevsky 2017). ∎

Lemma 7.2 (Learning Contraction in KL). Under Assumption A1, a single Bayesian update with learning rate \alpha_t contracts the KL divergence to the true posterior by a factor (1 - \alpha_t \rho).

Proof. The Langevin diffusion with LSI target converges exponentially in KL divergence (Vempala & Wibisono 2019, Theorem 1). The discrete‑time Bayesian update is a proximal step on the KL divergence and inherits this contraction rate (Altschuler & Chewi 2024, Proposition 1). ∎

Proof of (i). We construct a Lyapunov function

V(t) = E(t) + \gamma \sum_{i=1}^N D_{\text{KL}}(p^* \| p_i^t),

with \gamma > 0 chosen small enough that the contraction from learning dominates the perturbation from communication. Using Lemmas 7.1 and 7.2, we compute

\mathbb{E}[V(t+1)] \le (1 - \eta_t) V(t) + O(\delta),

where \eta_t = \min(\varepsilon \lambda_2, \gamma \alpha_t \rho). For \omega < \omega^*, the effective contraction rate \eta_t is positive, and the noise term is bounded. By the Robbins–Siegmund theorem (Robbins & Siegmund 1971), V(t) \to 0 almost surely. ∎

Proof of (ii) — Bifurcation Analysis. We perform a normal‑form analysis for the two‑agent Gaussian case. Let each agent's belief be p_i = \mathcal{N}(\mu_i, \sigma^2) with \sigma^2 fixed. The belief state is (\mu_1, \mu_2)^\top. The mean of the two agents evolves as:

\mu_{\text{mean}} = \frac{\mu_1 + \mu_2}{2}, \quad \mu_{\text{diff}} = \frac{\mu_1 - \mu_2}{2}.

The consensus step sets \mu_{\text{diff}} \to (1 - \varepsilon) \mu_{\text{diff}} (contractive). The Bayesian update with likelihood \mathcal{N}(\mu^*, \tau^2) pulls each mean toward \mu^*. For small \mu_{\text{diff}}, the coupled dynamics reduce to:

\begin{aligned}
\dot{\mu}_{\text{mean}} &= -\alpha (\mu_{\text{mean}} - \mu^*) + \beta \omega \mu_{\text{mean}} (\mu_{\text{mean}}^2 - \mu_c^2), \\
\dot{\mu}_{\text{diff}} &= -\varepsilon \omega \mu_{\text{diff}},
\end{aligned}

where \beta depends on the likelihood curvature and \mu_c is a critical value. The equation for \mu_{\text{mean}} is the normal form of a supercritical pitchfork bifurcation with parameter \omega. The Jacobian of the deterministic part at the symmetric fixed point \mu_1 = \mu_2 = \mu^* is

J = \begin{pmatrix}
-\alpha + \beta \omega (\mu^*)^2 & 0 \\
0 & -\varepsilon \omega
\end{pmatrix}.

The eigenvalue in the \mu_{\text{mean}} direction crosses zero when \omega = \omega^* = \alpha / (\beta (\mu^*)^2). For \omega > \omega^*, the symmetric fixed point becomes a saddle, and two new stable fixed points \pm \mu_c appear. These correspond to the malignant herding attractor: both agents confidently hold the same false belief.

The extension to N agents follows by centre‑manifold reduction (Carr 1981). The N-agent system has a slow manifold parameterised by the population mean, with identical normal form to the two‑agent case. ∎

