8. Theorem 2 — Stability of Ethical Attractors

8.1 Setup

Let the cognitive‑behavioural state be x \in \Omega_\varepsilon \subset H^1(\Omega), where \Omega_\varepsilon = \{x : c(x) \le \varepsilon\} is the ethical constraint set. The objective functional J(x) = \mathcal{I}_{\text{coll}}(x) is C^2 on \Omega_\varepsilon. The unconstrained dynamics are the gradient ascent

\dot{x} = \nabla J(x),

where \nabla J denotes the Fréchet gradient in H^{-1}(\Omega). The constrained dynamics are the projection of the gradient flow onto the tangent cone of \Omega_\varepsilon:

\dot{x} = \Pi_{T_{\Omega_\varepsilon}(x)}(\nabla J(x)).

The architectural halting constraint (Definition 6.1) is assumed to be in force, guaranteeing that the projection is well‑defined and that the flow never exits \Omega_\varepsilon.

8.2 Statement

Theorem 2. Under the above conditions:

· (i) There exists at least one Lyapunov‑stable equilibrium x^* \in \Omega_\varepsilon.
· (ii) The equilibrium is globally asymptotically stable within \Omega_\varepsilon if J is strictly concave on \Omega_\varepsilon.
· (iii) The global safety property c(x) \le \varepsilon is locally verifiable in time polynomial in N, while the corresponding global verification for a centralised system is coNP‑complete (Melo et al. 2025). Hence the distributed architecture with halting constraint makes alignment tractable.

8.3 Proof

Part (i) — Existence. Since \Omega_\varepsilon is compact in the weak topology of H^1(\Omega) (bounded sets in H^1 are relatively compact in L^2), and J is weakly upper semicontinuous (as a concave functional), the maximum is attained. Let x^* be a maximiser.

Part (i) — Lyapunov Stability. Define the Lyapunov functional

V(x) = J(x^*) - J(x).

Clearly V(x^*) = 0 and V(x) > 0 for x \neq x^* in a neighbourhood of x^*. The time derivative along the constrained flow is

\frac{dV}{dt} = -\langle \nabla J(x), \dot{x} \rangle_{H^{-1}, H^1} = -\langle \nabla J(x), \Pi_{T_{\Omega_\varepsilon}(x)}(\nabla J(x)) \rangle.

By the property of the projection onto a convex cone, the inner product is non‑negative: \langle \nabla J, \Pi_{T} (\nabla J) \rangle \ge 0. Hence dV/dt \le 0, with equality only when \nabla J(x) = 0, i.e., at x = x^*. This satisfies the Lyapunov stability criteria in the function‑space setting (Henry 1981).

Part (ii) — Global Convergence. If J is strictly concave, the gradient is a strictly monotone operator, and the projected gradient flow converges globally to the unique maximiser (Brézis 1973, Theorem 3.11). The halting constraint ensures that noise perturbations cannot eject the state from \Omega_\varepsilon, preserving global asymptotic stability.

Part (iii) — Complexity. By Theorem 6.1 (Melo et al. 2025), global verification of c(x) \le \varepsilon for a centralised system of sufficient expressiveness is coNP‑complete, via reduction from the complement of 3‑SAT. In the distributed architecture, the halting constraint decomposes c(x) \le \varepsilon into \bigwedge_i c_i(x_i) \le \varepsilon_i. Each local check is polynomial‑time by design, and the conjunction of N polynomial‑time checks is polynomial‑time. The decomposition is valid because the halting constraint ensures that if any local condition is violated, the global condition cannot hold. ∎

