11. Consistency Lemma

The four theorems form a closed logical cycle. Theorem 1 establishes that epistemic convergence requires sparse communication (\omega < \omega^*). Theorem 3 establishes that collective intelligence requires sufficient density (\rho > \rho_c). These two conditions pull in opposite directions: high density facilitates frequent communication, which risks malignant herding.

Lemma 11.1 (Existence of the Goldilocks Zone). There exists a non‑empty region in the (\rho, \omega) parameter space where Theorems 1 and 3 are simultaneously satisfied:

\mathcal{G} = \left\{ (\rho, \omega) : \rho > \rho_c,\; \omega < \omega^*(\rho) \right\} \neq \varnothing.

Proof. The critical communication frequency \omega^* depends inversely on the noise \delta and directly on the graph spectral gap \lambda_2. For random geometric graphs with communication radius r, the algebraic connectivity scales as \lambda_2 \sim r^2 (Boyd et al. 2006). If the communication radius is chosen adaptively such that agents communicate only with those whose cognitive maps overlap sufficiently, \omega^* remains bounded below even as \rho increases. Hence the Goldilocks zone is non‑empty. ∎

Theorem 4 then shows that within this zone, \mathcal{I} is maximised at an interior \kappa, providing a precise optimisation target. Theorem 2 guarantees that the resulting configuration is ethically stable and computationally verifiable.

